title: "GPIE — Green Policy Intelligence Engine: Independently Verifying Environmental Policy Claims Using Satellite Data"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/GPIE"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/GPIE"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21756661"
    description: "Zenodo archive of GPIE v1.2.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21756661"
abstract: >-
  GPIE is a geospatial causal-inference framework that tests whether the European
  Climate Law (Regulation (EU) 2021/1119, effective 30 June 2021) produced a
  statistically distinguishable reduction in tropospheric NO2 pollution across the
  EU-27, using Sentinel-5P TROPOMI satellite observations (2019-2024) and a
  Difference-in-Differences causal-inference design with a genuine 9-country non-EU
  control group (United Kingdom, Norway, Switzerland, Iceland, Albania, Bosnia and
  Herzegovina, Montenegro, North Macedonia, Serbia). The corrected two-group model
  finds no statistically significant pooled EU-wide effect at the conventional 5%
  level (p = 0.101, cluster-robust standard errors), but a heterogeneity check
  finds a statistically significant reduction concentrated in higher-baseline,
  more industrialized EU member states (p = 0.003), corroborated by a 23-quarter
  event-study analysis, a series of additional robustness checks, and an augmented
  synthetic control (weighted 7-country donor pool) reaching the same near-zero
  pooled estimate through a structurally independent method. A Moran's I
  spatial-autocorrelation test confirms raw NO2 levels are strongly spatially
  clustered (I=0.570, p=0.001) but the DiD model's residuals are not significantly
  clustered (I=0.069, p=0.135), consistent with the country and month fixed effects
  absorbing most of that dependence. Applying the same design to a secondary NDVI
  (vegetation health) outcome finds a statistically significant relative decline
  (p = 0.007), reported as an exploratory finding.
keywords:
  - causal inference
  - difference-in-differences
  - synthetic control
  - spatial autocorrelation
  - treatment effect heterogeneity
  - satellite remote sensing
  - Sentinel-5P
  - air pollution
  - NO2
  - NDVI
  - European Green Deal
  - policy evaluation
  - geospatial analysis
